      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1,\n\\]\nwe first need to identify the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), where \\(a^2\\) and \\(b^2\\) are the denominators of \\(y^2\\) and \\(x^2\\) terms respectively.\n\nHere, \\(a^2 = 27\\) and \\(b^2 = 11\\). Hence,\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{27} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = \\sqrt{11}.\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is given by \\(2a\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times \\sqrt{27} = 2 \\times \\sqrt{9 \\times 3} = 2 \\times 3 \\times \\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nTo confirm the accuracy, let's use Python to calculate this value.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given values\na_squared = 27\nb_squared = 11\n\n# Calculating a and b\na = math.sqrt(a_squared)\nb = math.sqrt(b_squared)\n\n# Distance between the vertices\ndistance_between_vertices = 2 * a\nprint(distance_between_vertices)\n```\n```output\n10.392304845413264\n```\nThe calculated distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is \\(6\\sqrt{3}\\), which is approximately 10.39. Hence, the exact distance is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
