      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1,\n\\]\nwe need to identify the standard form of the hyperbola and understand its key features.\n\nThe standard form of a hyperbola that opens vertically (i.e., its transverse axis is vertical) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1.\n\\]\nThis form indicates that \\(a^2\\) is the denominator under \\(y^2\\), and \\(b^2\\) is the denominator under \\(x^2\\).\n\nGiven the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1,\n\\]\nwe can compare it to the standard form to extract the values of \\(a^2\\) and \\(b^2\\):\n- \\(a^2 = 27\\) implies \\(a = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\).\n- \\(b^2 = 11\\) implies \\(b = \\sqrt{11}\\).\n\nThe vertices of a hyperbola in this form are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). Thus, the vertices are at:\n- \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\)\n- \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\)\n\nThe distance between these two vertices is the horizontal distance between the \\(y\\)-coordinates \\(3\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(-3\\sqrt{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = |a| = 3\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\sqrt{3}}.\n\\]",
